n": 6.0, "environment": 4.05, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.95, "baseline_margin": 17.96, "analyst_adjustment": 0.04, "key_factors": ["Rhode Island's strong Democratic lean (D+8 PVI) in an open-seat race", "All six expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic", "Polls show the Democrat leading by 22-27 points with the Republican stuck near 15-18%", "Prediction markets price the Democrat at 96-97% to win"], "rationale": "Foulkes enters the general after unseating McKee in the primary, and every available signal points Democratic: raters unanimously call it Safe or Solid D, late-August polls show a 22-27 point lead, and markets price her near 96%. The fundamentals-based margin of about 10 points is the conservative anchor, but the polling and rating consensus justify a mid-to-high-teens expected margin.", "watch": "Independent candidates Ken Block and CD Reynolds could siphon anti-establishment votes, and lingering controversy over Foulkes's returned excess donations bears monitoring.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governor's race is an open-seat contest after Helena Foulkes defeated incumbent Dan McKee in the September Democratic primary. She faces Republican nominee Aaron Guckian, a former state official who won a contested GOP primary, along with several independents including Ken Block. The state's Democratic lean of about eight points gives Foulkes a strong structural advantage.\n\nEvery expert rater calls the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and late-August polls from UNH and Emerson show Foulkes ahead by 22 to 27 points, with Guckian polling in the mid-to-high teens. Prediction markets price a Democratic win at roughly 96 to 97 percent. The main open questions are the final margin and whether independent candidates can consolidate enough anti-establishment sentiment to tighten the race.", "history": [{"date": "2